+The protocol supplies maps $Q,W_{\mathrm{on}}:\mathcal P_s\to\mathbb R$ whose values are interpreted in joules as in [[def-td-si-quantities]] and whose energy bookkeeping uses $g_s$. $Q[P]>0$ means energy enters the system through the declared thermal-transfer channel; $W_{\mathrm{on}}[P]>0$ means mechanical work is done on the system. These are functionals of the process record, not state functions of $x_P^-$ and $x_P^+$. Heat is the protocol-assigned thermal mode of energy transfer, not a quantity stored in the equilibrium state. The protocol additionally supplies a predicate $a_s:\mathcal P_s\to\{0,1\}$ recording absence of thermal transfer: $a_s(P)=1$ means that no thermal energy crosses the selected outer boundary through any thermal-transfer channel throughout $I_P$. This condition concerns the full control/contact and transfer records, including all channels and both transfer directions; it is supplied and checked as part of the declared model, not inferred from the net map $Q$. Require the consistency condition $a_s(P)=1\Rightarrow Q[P]=0$. A record is adiabatic exactly when $a_s(P)=1$. The broader class $\{P:Q[P]=0\}$ is the zero-net-heat class; a record that absorbs and releases equal nonzero heat belongs to that class and is not adiabatic. No continuity, differentiability, or particular heat-flux representation is required to supply this predicate. On $\mathcal P_{\mathrm{cl}}(s)$, use the restrictions of these same maps; defining them on the larger class supplies no energy-balance law for records outside the core class. A closed record may still exchange energy; it is not thereby isolated.
 
 A preparation is a model-specified rule selecting the particular initial equilibrium state $x_P^-$. A statistical extension may instead specify a normalized probability measure on a declared measurable space, as in [[def-probability-measure]]; such a statistical preparation is outside this deterministic process class. A preparation does not assert later equilibration. An admitted record may pass through nonequilibrium configurations even though both endpoints lie in $D_c$.
